KsxYgqJheqAQJ2aJHuB6T2ZhHz1_51haI0c05BZ7NM6HJkdqtyD9YNuU4YUc4hI7lb0vO5l2He4=
CEXsauv1-7vE1Ri60KgpIJSEdTH4_CrKNro8TqXBvZLdQ7iZJG--hVWEUsxFZGAYSZ42jtz_Jtr=
Mtd9Q8xS7zhI0d61l6bq6rZ29kmXmNOv7SAXuQQZNDIB7HiXkfPvl6vNag8hRTBS-oGbereuXOw=
MOxHU5T/4vo/XaP5KdSbSLaPvsfiI9mfJw/t10/h001.PcmaQdbiFVaadSF4x0ZTZbIZVy_UnRY=
huxsMIaOGj0c
Emma Kelly
, who plays for Newcastle United, unexpectedly became pregnant earlier this=
 year at the age of 29, she was anxious. She had never played with a pregna=
nt teammate before and she had been out for much of the season due to injur=
y. How isolating would the experience be? Was it going to jeopardise her ca=
reer? =E2=80=9CI was questioning whether this is the right time,=E2=80=9D s=
he says. =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s not that I=E2=80=99m ungrateful but it=E2=80=
=99s just finding it hard being out of the team again and not being able to=
 play the sport that I love.=E2=80=9D


Newcastle=E2=80=99s Emma Kelly became pregnant earlier this year. Photograp=
h: Michelle Mercer/Newcastle United/Getty Images


Though she continued to train, she and her partner decided she would stop c=
ontact football immediately, prior to 12 weeks, as =E2=80=9Cfootball is so =
unpredictable=E2=80=9D, she says. As she didn=E2=80=99t want to announce he=
r pregnancy until she was into the second trimester, in the interim she tol=
d her teammates that she was unwell. Like Emslie she has been
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR9NJ=
KsxYgqJheqAQJ2aJHuAU6loy5DS9MXX_ajii7TdlHIoLnUSlcgWogwDDe8uieFHdZ7vuRH7E06_=
wvH6CbJqOYTM4sPUJdmxjc07kecLAUmMrUSZZoeFtAJ6L8MCph2w-lX11Lt9uo0dWl53UT_X1rS=
BPexQbTVMLLQqWQzeoqf2S4Iz7BTDVrgiEe1zbsObC1cGuI3UTEwuaY1oiQJI1vbXLPj-htbS-Z=
qi0-3dR/4vo/XaP5KdSbSLaPvsfiI9mfJw/t11/h001.CAeStO70FCVOZMGtiMsXC9k1eTqTCgw=
AHW4fLCwceEU
documenting her training
with the encouragement of her club and feels as if she is noticing more and=
 more athletes doing the same. =E2=80=9CI want to continue as long as my bo=
dy allows me to,=E2=80=9D she says. Her club has been =E2=80=9Cextremely su=
pportive=E2=80=9D with specialised training, physio and equipment.

But for all the newfound positivity around pre-and postpartum for footballe=
rs, Emslie says candidly, there are some hard truths that players have to f=
ace: namely that pregnancy is a sacrifice. The pitch is their stage and no =
player wants to be relegated to the role of understudy, relearning how to b=
end their body to their will. =E2=80=9CIf I wasn=E2=80=99t worried about [f=
ertility], I wouldn=E2=80=99t have gotten pregnant till I was retired,=E2=
=80=9D she says. =E2=80=9CI could have had a full football career.=E2=80=9D

The other is that football is a business. =E2=80=9CYou=E2=80=99re used as a=
 player,=E2=80=9D she says bluntly. =E2=80=9CAs soon as you turn a certain =
age, or if you=E2=80=99ve got a kid, they look at you different =E2=80=A6 A=
s a player, you=E2=80=99re wanted until you=E2=80=99re not.=E2=80=9D Though=
 she made it clear she wasn=E2=80=99t talking about her own club, which was=
 very supportive of her pregnancy and return, at the time of interview she =
was two months away from her contract ending with Angel City but had no new=
s of a new deal being offered.

Even so, Dufour says, the body works hard during pregnancy; aerobic capacit=
y is pushed. =E2=80=9CThe biggest stress test you=E2=80=99re ever going to =
go through is pregnancy,=E2=80=9D she says. Her data shows that some women =
will achieve personal bests on their return. In short pregnancy could be a =
secret superpower. In an industry as capitalistic, brutal and data-driven a=
s football, that=E2=80=99s an important fact.

